/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 332 0 R
/T (Problem Statement  The fundamental crisis in IoT security stems from a radical asymmetry between the capabilities of modern attackers and the defensive resources available to embedded devices. As of 2024, approximately 70% of IoT devices exhibit significant vulnerabilities, ranging from unpatched firmware and weak authentication to the complete absence of encryption for telemetry data. This vulnerability is compounded by several critical factors that render traditional security models inadequate for current and future deployments. )
/E (Problem Statement  The fundamental crisis in IoT security stems from a radical asymmetry between the capabilities of modern attackers and the defensive resources available to embedded devices. As of 2024, approximately 70% of IoT devices exhibit significant vulnerabilities, ranging from unpatched firmware and weak authentication to the complete absence of encryption for telemetry data. This vulnerability is compounded by several critical factors that render traditional security models inadequate for current and future deployments. )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
359 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 332 0 R
/T (Resource constraints remain the primary barrier to robust encryption. Standard algorithms like AES-128 or RSA-2048 impose substantial computational and memory overheads that can lead to increased latency in real-time systems, such as medical heart-rate monitors or industrial controllers. In the clinical context of the Internet of Medical Things \(IoMT\), high-latency encryption can disrupt the reliability of critical alerts, potentially compromising patient safety. Moreover, the energy consumption required for complex cryptographic operations can rapidly deplete the battery life of remote sensors, leading to maintenance failures and data loss. )
/E (Resource constraints remain the primary barrier to robust encryption. Standard algorithms like AES-128 or RSA-2048 impose substantial computational and memory overheads that can lead to increased latency in real-time systems, such as medical heart-rate monitors or industrial controllers. In the clinical context of the Internet of Medical Things \(IoMT\), high-latency encryption can disrupt the reliability of critical alerts, potentially compromising patient safety. Moreover, the energy consumption required for complex cryptographic operations can rapidly deplete the battery life of remote sensors, leading to maintenance failures and data loss. )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
360 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 332 0 R
/T (Current research also highlights the rising threat of Side-Channel Attacks \(SCA\), which exploit physical signals such as power fluctuations, electromagnetic radiation, and timing variances to extract secret keys. Mathematical security does not guarantee implementation security; a mathematically "unbreakable" cipher may still leak its key through the physical characteristics of the hardware it runs on. This is particularly problematic for lightweight cryptographic \(LWC\) primitives implemented in unrolled architectures, where low latency is prioritized over side-channel resistance. )
/E (Current research also highlights the rising threat of Side-Channel Attacks \(SCA\), which exploit physical signals such as power fluctuations, electromagnetic radiation, and timing variances to extract secret keys. Mathematical security does not guarantee implementation security; a mathematically "unbreakable" cipher may still leak its key through the physical characteristics of the hardware it runs on. This is particularly problematic for lightweight cryptographic \(LWC\) primitives implemented in unrolled architectures, where low latency is prioritized over side-channel resistance. )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
361 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 332 0 R
/T (The "Harvest Now, Decrypt Later" threat model adds a temporal dimension to the problem. Adversaries are currently intercepting and archiving encrypted data with the expectation that future quantum computers, utilizing algorithms like Shor's or Grover's, will be able to break classical asymmetric and symmetric encryptions. This necessitates the immediate adoption of post-quantum robust or structurally complex encryption methods that do not rely solely on the hardness of integer factorization or discrete logarithms. )
/E (The "Harvest Now, Decrypt Later" threat model adds a temporal dimension to the problem. Adversaries are currently intercepting and archiving encrypted data with the expectation that future quantum computers, utilizing algorithms like Shor's or Grover's, will be able to break classical asymmetric and symmetric encryptions. This necessitates the immediate adoption of post-quantum robust or structurally complex encryption methods that do not rely solely on the hardness of integer factorization or discrete logarithms. )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
362 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 332 0 R
/T (Finally, the lack of adaptive security frameworks means that most IoT devices operate with a static security posture, regardless of the actual threat level or device status. A device with a low battery in a low-threat environment may still be forced to run high-overhead encryption, while a device under active attack may lack the intelligence to strengthen its security parameters dynamically. There is a critical need for a hybrid approach that integrates structural graph-based security with machine learning to provide an adaptive, resource-efficient, and quantum-ready defense for network-based IoT data exchange. )
/E (Finally, the lack of adaptive security frameworks means that most IoT devices operate with a static security posture, regardless of the actual threat level or device status. A device with a low battery in a low-threat environment may still be forced to run high-overhead encryption, while a device under active attack may lack the intelligence to strengthen its security parameters dynamically. There is a critical need for a hybrid approach that integrates structural graph-based security with machine learning to provide an adaptive, resource-efficient, and quantum-ready defense for network-based IoT data exchange. )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
