/T (Machine Learning Integration and Anomaly Detection  The second phase of the methodology involves embedding machine learning into the security lifecycle. The Autoencoder model is used to generate the seeds for the IDG positioning. During the training phase, the model learns to compress and reconstruct complex patterns, and the resulting latent representation is hashed \(e.g., using SHA-512\) to produce a 512-bit seed. This seed is then used by both the sender \(Node A\) and receiver \(Node B\) to derive identical graph labeling sequences without transmitting the key itself. )
/E (Machine Learning Integration and Anomaly Detection  The second phase of the methodology involves embedding machine learning into the security lifecycle. The Autoencoder model is used to generate the seeds for the IDG positioning. During the training phase, the model learns to compress and reconstruct complex patterns, and the resulting latent representation is hashed \(e.g., using SHA-512\) to produce a 512-bit seed. This seed is then used by both the sender \(Node A\) and receiver \(Node B\) to derive identical graph labeling sequences without transmitting the key itself. )
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
384 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 335 0 R
/T (Concurrently, an Artificial Neural Network \(ANN\) is deployed at the network broker to monitor MQTT traffic. The model is trained on a standardized IoT dataset \(e.g., MQTTset\) which includes legitimate traffic and five common attack vectors: DoS, brute-force, malformed packets, SlowITe, and flooding. The ANN architecture consists of an input layer for traffic features, multiple hidden layers optimized through trial-and-error, and an output layer for multi-class classification. This model functions as a real-time gatekeeper, granting access only to connections that do not exhibit malicious signatures. )
/E (Concurrently, an Artificial Neural Network \(ANN\) is deployed at the network broker to monitor MQTT traffic. The model is trained on a standardized IoT dataset \(e.g., MQTTset\) which includes legitimate traffic and five common attack vectors: DoS, brute-force, malformed packets, SlowITe, and flooding. The ANN architecture consists of an input layer for traffic features, multiple hidden layers optimized through trial-and-error, and an output layer for multi-class classification. This model functions as a real-time gatekeeper, granting access only to connections that do not exhibit malicious signatures. )
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [2]
>>
